- `src/back/objects/domain/SettingsService.py`
- `src/back/objects/session/GlobalConfigService.py`
- `src/back/core/helpers/DatabricksHelpers.py`
- `src/front/templates/settings.html`
- `src/front/static/config/js/settings.js`
- `src/front/config/menu_config.json`
- `tests/units/auth/test_oidc_host_split.py` (new)
- `tests/units/settings/test_global_config_without_volume.py` (new)
- `tests/units/auth/test_oidc_flow.py`
- `tests/units/front/test_backend_brand_icons.py`
- `tests/units/settings/test_graph_engine_config.py`
- `tests/units/settings/test_delta_warehouse_config.py`

### Test result

`uv run --frozen pytest -q -m "not scenario"` → **5055 passed, 1 failed,
295 skipped**. Same pre-existing NLTK `wordnet` failure.

## Make the Databricks workspace host configurable in the UI

### Context

The host was a read-only plaintext div captioned "Set via environment variable
DATABRICKS_HOST", so correcting it required a redeploy. That is the wrong shape
for this particular setting, because the wrong value is easy to choose and fails
*silently*: an account console — or a vanity domain in front of one — answers
OIDC discovery perfectly and returns HTTP 303 for every `/api/2.0/...` path, so
login succeeds while every Unity Catalog and SQL warehouse call redirects into
nothing. That is exactly what happened on this deployment.

### Changes

1. `src/front/templates/settings.html` — the host is an input with a **Save**
   button, carrying the same "Admin only – applies to all users" badge as the
   warehouse picker. The help text gives the per-workspace URL shape, says the
   `?o=` number in the browser URL is the workspace id, and states that an
   account console will be rejected and why.
2. `src/back/objects/domain/SettingsService.py` —
   - `probe_workspace_host` verifies a host before accepting it. **A 401/403 is
     the pass condition** (a real workspace API answered and wants credentials);
     a 3xx is the failure, and the rejection names the account-vs-workspace
     mistake with the URL shape to use instead. Network errors and 5xx warn but
     do not block, since a private-endpoint workspace can be unreachable from
     wherever the probe runs while being fine from the app.
   - `set_workspace_host` gates on admin, normalises the value (adds `https://`,
     strips a trailing slash), persists globally, and also writes the session so
     it applies immediately when the registry write fails. Empty clears the
     override and skips the probe.
   - `/settings/current` gained `host_source` (`session` / `global` / `env` /
     `unset`) and `host_env_default`, so the panel distinguishes "this is just
     the deployment default" from "an admin overrode it".
3. `src/back/objects/session/GlobalConfigService.py` — `get_workspace_host` /
   `set_workspace_host`. The getter deliberately takes no host/token: it is
   consulted *while* resolving the host, so it cannot require one. Safe because
   `_store_for` discards both and reads Postgres.
4. `src/back/core/helpers/DatabricksHelpers.py` — resolution is now
   **session → global config → `DATABRICKS_HOST`**. No recursion: the global
   config read reaches the Postgres registry, which needs no Databricks host.
   `_global_workspace_host` never raises, so a registry that is down falls
   through to the environment variable.
5. `src/api/routers/internal/settings.py` — `POST /settings/workspace-host`.
6. `src/front/static/config/js/settings.js` — hydration, source caption, and a
   save handler that renders `message` (not the machine error code, so the
   account-host diagnosis survives to the screen).

### The login issuer stays an environment variable

`ONTOBRICKS_OIDC_HOST` is deliberately **not** UI-settable, and there is a test
asserting no route accepts it. It is needed to authenticate before anyone can
reach this page, so a wrong value stored behind its own login would be
unrecoverable without a redeploy — the exact problem this change removes for the
workspace host. The panel says so explicitly.

### Tests

`tests/units/settings/test_workspace_host_configurable.py` (new, 21) — the probe
verdict for 301/302/303/307/308 vs 401/403 vs unreachable vs 5xx, that the
rejection names the real mistake, admin gating, global vs session-fallback
scoping, normalisation, clearing, that clearing does not probe, and that the OIDC
issuer is not writable here. **6 fail against a permissive probe.**

`tests/units/api/test_ui_rendering.py` — `test_host_display` asserted the
read-only div. Replaced with `test_host_is_editable` plus a check that the panel
names both hosts.

### Modified files

- `src/back/objects/domain/SettingsService.py`
- `src/back/objects/session/GlobalConfigService.py`
- `src/back/core/helpers/DatabricksHelpers.py`
- `src/api/routers/internal/settings.py`
- `src/front/templates/settings.html`
- `src/front/static/config/js/settings.js`
- `tests/units/settings/test_workspace_host_configurable.py` (new)
- `tests/units/api/test_ui_rendering.py`

### Test result

`uv run --frozen pytest -q -m "not scenario"` → **5077 passed, 1 failed,
295 skipped**. Same pre-existing NLTK `wordnet` failure.

## Two dead ends in the Settings UI, and the mess around them

### Context

Two things the panel asked for were impossible to do:

* Every save was refused with *"Only admins (CAN MANAGE) can change the SQL
  Warehouse"* — on a page showing the user's own **Admin** badge in the sidebar.
* The Registry panel said *"Click **Initialize** to create the registry tables"*
  while having hidden that very button.

Both are the same failure as everything else this release: a check keyed on
Databricks Apps / Unity Catalog facts that no longer hold, with the *other* half
of the UI reading from the source that does.

### Changes

1. **`PermissionService.is_admin` — the registry is now the authority.** It read
   only `CAN_MANAGE` on the Databricks App ACL
   (`GET /api/2.0/permissions/apps/<name>`). Off the Apps platform there is no
   app to hold an ACL, so all three paths returned "could not determine",
   `bool(None)` became `False`, and **nobody could be an admin for writes** no
   matter what `app_roles` said — which is precisely the table the sidebar's
   Admin badge is read from. `app_roles` is consulted first; the ACL remains a
   fallback for deployments still on the platform. An empty `app_name` no longer
   short-circuits before the role lookup.
   `require_admin_error` had resolved `registry_cfg` and then discarded it,
   which is why only the ACL was ever reachable; it passes it now. The message
   no longer cites `CAN MANAGE` (an Apps ACL level) or names the SQL Warehouse
   whatever setting was actually refused — it says who to ask instead.
2. **The Initialize button follows PostgreSQL, not the Volume.**
   `updateRegistryLabel` hid it in the `catalog && schema` else-branch. It
   creates the *PostgreSQL* registry tables, so it is now gated on
   `postgres.bound`. The Volume labels stay gated on the triplet, since they
   describe the Volume, and read "No Volume configured (optional)" rather than
   "Not configured".
3. **Save failures are readable.** Per-field errors were joined with `\n` into a
   notification that renders as HTML, so six identical refusals became one
   unbroken wall of text. `summarizeSaveErrors` groups identical reasons and
   lists the affected fields.
4. **The workspace-host help text is trimmed.** Three dense paragraphs went into
   the panel in the previous commit; that was too much and wrapped badly. One
   line now, with the rest behind a **Details** toggle.
5. **Registry panel copy.** "Lakebase" → "PostgreSQL" in the heading, the
   connection card and the permission-check card; "Connection parameters come
   from the Databricks App database resource binding … The OAuth token is minted
   automatically" now describes the `PG*` environment; three "from app resource
   binding" captions now say "from the PG* environment"; the lock note reads
   "Configured from the deployment environment".
6. **A callout told operators to run `scripts/migrate-registry-to-lakebase.sh`**,
   deleted with the bundle in v0.7.1. Removed, along with two backend docstrings
   citing it.

### Tests

- `tests/units/registry/test_admin_from_app_roles.py` (new, 9) — an `app_roles`
  admin is admin with no Databricks app and no `app_name`; non-admin roles are
  not; the ACL still grants and denies as a fallback; legacy callers with no
  `registry_cfg` still reach it; the denial message cites neither `CAN MANAGE`
  nor the SQL Warehouse. **2 fail with the ACL authoritative again.**
- `tests/units/front/test_frontend_canonical_values.py` —
  `TestActionableEmptyStates` (4).

  Worth recording: the first version of the Initialize test **passed against the
  reverted bug**. It scanned a window of source for "postgres" and "bound", and
  the explanatory comment I had just written contained both words. It was
  rewritten to assert the guard *expression* assigned to
  `initBtn.style.display`, plus a third test that `pgBound` is derived from the
  payload rather than folded to a constant. Both controls now fail: 2 when the
  Volume gate returns, 1 when `pgBound` becomes `true`.

### Modified files

- `src/back/objects/registry/PermissionService.py`
- `src/back/objects/domain/SettingsService.py`
- `src/back/objects/registry/store/__init__.py`
- `src/front/static/registry/js/registry.js`
- `src/front/static/config/js/settings.js`
